# The MIP set — 17 instances from MIPLIB 3, pinned (D289).
#
# What it asks: does branch and bound reach the published integer optimum,
# is the point it publishes integral and feasible to the checker, and do
# two cold searches build the same tree. Nothing in the three gate sets
# can ask any of that, because none of them has an integer column. Not a
# gate set: run it whenever src/mip.c or anything under it changes, and
# read `nodes=` beside `work=`, since a tree that changed shape with the
# answer unmoved is what the digest cannot show.
#
#   make miplib J=12
#
# The 17 are the members of MIPLIB 3 (Bixby, Ceria, McZeal, Savelsbergh,
# Optima 58, 1998) that the plain tree of D288 solves inside 60 s on
# this host, out of 38 tried; the rest are in
# bench/measurements/02-189/ with what each cost. Served by ZIB's mirror
# at miplib2010.zib.de/miplib3/miplib3 as plain gzipped MPS, so the
# pipeline is mps-gz and no expander is needed.
#
# Format matches netlib.manifest so one parser serves every set:
#
#     name  sha256  rows  cols  reference  source  objconst
#
# sha256 is of the served .mps.gz. rows and cols are the catalogue's
# (miplib.cat in the mirror's tarball), which count constraints without
# the objective row, as JAOS does. reference is the catalogue's INT SOLN
# column; two entries carry more digits than the catalogue prints, since
# its rounding of rgn (82.1999) is outside the runner's 1e-6 and egout
# (568.101) inside it by a hair: 82.19999924 and 568.1007 are MIPLIB
# 2010's figures for the same files and what every search here reaches.
# objconst is 0 throughout; none of these carries an objective constant.
#
